rything inside the run method will  now be executed on our new thread now  let's create our display inside the run  method will type in display dot set  display mode to new display mode with  comma height we'll have to import  display mode so hover your mouse over it  and make sure you import the lwg l1 not  the java one this line will set up the  size of our window to the width and  height we specified above now it looks  like we've got one big error but don't  worry  setting your display mode simply throws  an exception which we now have to handle  hover your mouse over that line and  slice around with try and catch there  you go all good inside the try block  lists at the title of our window - the  title variable flappy that we created  before and finally let's call display it  or create to create our display that's  most of the code done simple isn't it  now let's make our main game loop our  game will have to repeat certain events  as long as it's running we'll have to  keep updating our display by calling  display to update and if the user closes  the window we need to set running equal  to false if that happens our while loop  will be history  and we'll need to destroy our display to  close the window now we're ready to see  if our code worked  let's call new main dot start which will  create a new instance of our main class  and then call the start method which of  course will start our game the red dot  start will call our run method and we'll  get into our loop until we close the  window note that we can't simply call  the start method by itself since the  main method is static and our start  method is not let's launch our game by  clicking on the debug button and see  what happens great black screen which  seems to be the size we want it and has  the correct title we've succeeded  unfortunately that's not all well this  will probably work on all platforms Mac  and Linux by default we'll use opengl  2.1 or lower whilst windows will  typically use the highest version  available opengl 2.1 is too low for us  we want 3.3 so how do we get it we  request it before we create the display  we'll need to create a context  attributes object and set it equal to  three three since we want opengl 3.3  let's import contacts and trips  differently than what we did last time  we'll use the keyboard shortcut ctrl  shift o or command shift o if you're on  Mac to let Eclipse organize our imports  let's select the OpenGL context trips  rather than OpenGL ES and click finish  organized imports will import all  required classes that we're currently  using and is generally a much quicker  way to import classes it will also  remove any imports that we're not using  pretty neat now when we create our  display will create a new pixel format  and pass in our context object along  with a statement with profile call true  the OpenGL call profile means that any  old deprecated code will essentially be  removed so we're stuck with only the  ability to use codes still supported in  three point three that's great since it  will actively stop us from writing  old-school code that should be enough  however there is currently a bug with  our l w GL requests an OpenGL contacts  from Mac and possibly Linux as well  requesting three point three gets us two  point one and requesting three point two  guess I'll say the three point three or  four point one depending on the computer  pretty bizarre so basically we need to  set our contacts to trips three point  two if the game runs on Mac note that  this is for version two point nine point  one or veil  so hopefully this bug will be fixed soon  so how can we check if we're on Mac  let's go ahead and type in an if  statement to check to see if a certain  system property called OS name contains  the string Mac if so we're on Mac and we  can go ahead and set the context equal  to 3.2 and now we're done let's check to  see that our window still works  right  I hope you guys enjoyed this video if  you did please don't forget to leave a  like I'll see you guys in the next  episode  well we'll be setting up OpenGL good bye

